        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A typical radon mitigation system, usually sub-slab depressurization with one pipe and one fan, costs roughly 800 to 2,500 dollars installed, and many quotes for an ordinary basement or slab home land somewhere around 1,000 to 2,000 dollars. After that, the fan typically adds a modest amount to your electric bill, often in the range of 50 to 150 dollars a year depending on the fan and local rates. Crawl spaces that need a sealed membrane, homes with several foundation types and finished basements cost more, sometimes well past 3,000 dollars. These are typical US ranges that vary by region and change over time. EPA recommends fixing your home if radon is 4 pCi/L or higher and considering it between 2 and 4, and it recommends a qualified contractor, a retest after installation and another test every couple of years.
        </p>

        <div id="cost-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Does Radon Mitigation Cost by Home Type?
          </h2>

          <p>
            EPA has long described the cost of fixing a radon problem as similar to other common home repairs, and the typical range for a standard system is roughly 800 to 2,500 dollars. Where you land in that range depends mainly on your foundation, how easily air moves under the slab, how many suction points are needed and how hard it is to route the pipe from the basement or slab to above the roof. A simple unfinished basement with gravel under the slab is the low end.
          </p>

          <p>
            Costs rise once the house gets more complicated. A home with a basement plus a crawl space addition needs suction in both areas. Dense soil or no gravel under the slab can mean extra suction points. Finished basements limit where pipes can go, and running a pipe through living space up to the attic involves more carpentry than running it outside along an exterior wall. Each of those choices shows up in the quote.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below shows typical ranges by home type. Use them as a guide for what is reasonable, not as a quote. Regional labor rates, state licensing requirements and local building code requirements, such as where the exhaust must terminate, all affect the final number. Prices have also moved with materials and labor costs, so the same job may cost more today than it did a few years ago.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
