          <p>
            Before getting any quotes, make sure you are working from a reliable test result. A single short-term test at a high level, especially above about 8 pCi/L, is usually enough to move ahead; results closer to 4 often justify a second test first. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-does-a-radon-test-take/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long a radon test takes</a> explains how to get a result you can act on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Does Sub-Slab Depressurization Work, and Why Is It the Default?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Radon is a gas from the natural breakdown of uranium in soil and rock. It enters a house because the air pressure inside is usually slightly lower than in the soil underneath, especially in winter, so the house draws soil gas in through cracks, joints, sump pits and other openings. Sub-slab depressurization reverses that. A fan creates a constant suction under the slab so that soil gas is pulled into a pipe and vented outside instead of being drawn into the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            The installer drills a hole of a few inches through the slab, digs out a small pit underneath to improve airflow, and connects a PVC pipe to it. The pipe runs to a fan located outside the living space, typically in an attic, garage or on an exterior wall, and then up to discharge above the roof line, away from windows and other openings. A simple gauge called a manometer on the pipe shows whether the system is pulling suction. Cracks, floor joints and sump lids are sealed so the fan pulls from the soil, not the basement.
          </p>

          <p>
            EPA identifies this kind of active soil depressurization as the most common and usually most reliable approach for homes with basements or slabs, and it can reduce radon levels dramatically. Sealing cracks alone is not enough; EPA notes that sealing by itself has not been shown to lower radon significantly or consistently. Sealers such as those in our <a href="/drylok-vs-radonseal/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">DRYLOK vs RadonSeal</a> comparison help with moisture and support a system, but they are not a radon fix on their own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Open sump pits are a common entry point, so mitigators typically cover them with a sealed, airtight lid, sometimes with the pit itself used as a suction point. A submersible pump makes that easy because it sits below a flat lid; a pedestal pump's column makes an airtight seal harder. Our comparison of <a href="/pedestal-vs-submersible-sump-pump/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pedestal vs submersible sump pumps</a> covers that tradeoff.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="factors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Makes a Radon System Cost More?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest technical factor is what lies under the slab. Gravel or coarse fill lets a single suction point reach the whole floor, which keeps the job simple. Packed clay, sand or a slab poured directly on dense soil restricts airflow, and the installer may need more suction points, a larger pit under the slab or a stronger fan. Many contractors run a quick diagnostic test of under-slab airflow before quoting to see how far suction reaches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pipe routing comes next. The cheapest route usually goes straight out through a rim joist and up the outside of the house. Some homeowners prefer an interior route through a closet or chase up to the attic for appearance, which costs more and involves cutting through floors. Local codes or state standards may dictate where the exhaust ends and where the fan can be located; fans are generally not installed inside the living space or a basement because a leak in the pressurized side could push radon indoors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Foundation complexity adds cost. Homes with additions on different foundations, crawl spaces that need membranes, hollow block walls that leak soil gas, or interior drain tile systems all need more thought. Some systems use the existing drain tile or sump pit as the suction point, which can be efficient. Finished basements raise prices because ceilings and walls limit pipe routes and patching may be needed afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Regional factors round it out: labor rates, state licensing, how much radon is common in your area and how busy contractors are. Areas with widespread high radon often have experienced installers and competitive pricing. Ask whether a quote includes the post-mitigation test, a warranty on the fan and on reduction results, and any permits. A quote that includes a guarantee to get below 4 pCi/L is worth more than a cheaper one that does not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="crawl-slab" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Does Mitigation Cost for Crawl Spaces and Slab Homes?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Crawl spaces with exposed soil are usually treated with sub-membrane depressurization. A heavy plastic sheet is laid over the soil, sealed at the seams and to the foundation walls, and a pipe from under the sheet connects to a fan. The fan pulls soil gas from beneath the membrane and vents it outside. Costs commonly run roughly 1,500 to 4,000 dollars or more, depending on the crawl space's size and height and the thickness and durability of the membrane.
          </p>

          <p>
            This overlaps with crawl space encapsulation, which seals the crawl space for moisture control with a heavier liner, sealed vents and often a dehumidifier. An encapsulation can be designed to serve as the membrane for a radon system, and doing both at once is usually cheaper than doing them separately. Our guide to <a href="/crawl-space-encapsulation-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">crawl space encapsulation cost</a> breaks down that larger job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Passive approaches, such as relying only on crawl space vents, can lower radon somewhat in some homes, but they are much less predictable than an active system. EPA's guidance generally favors active systems when levels need to come down reliably. If you open vents for radon, be aware of the moisture and energy tradeoffs, particularly in humid climates or cold winters where vent pipes and plumbing are at risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Slab-on-grade homes typically get the same sub-slab system as a basement, with the suction point drilled through the slab in a garage, closet or utility room. Costs run similar to basement systems, sometimes a little higher if the only practical routes go through finished living space. Homes with radiant heat or utilities in the slab need careful drilling, which a qualified installer will plan around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Does a Radon System Cost to Run and Maintain?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Radon fans run continuously, 24 hours a day. Typical residential fans draw on the order of tens of watts to around 100 or more, depending on the model and how hard it has to pull. At average US electricity rates, that usually works out to somewhere around 50 to 150 dollars a year. A smaller fan in a home with good sub-slab airflow sits at the low end; a high-suction fan for dense soil sits higher.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a small heating and cooling penalty, because the system pulls a little conditioned air from the house through any unsealed cracks. Careful sealing during installation minimizes that. In most homes it is a modest amount compared with the fan's electricity use, but it is one more reason a good installer seals the slab, joints and sump pit thoroughly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fans are the one part that wears out. Many last roughly 5 to 10 years or more, and manufacturer warranties commonly cover several years. Replacing a fan typically costs a few hundred dollars installed, less if you are comfortable swapping it yourself where that is allowed. Check the manometer every so often: if the liquid levels are even, the fan has likely stopped, and you should call your installer or replace it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget for periodic testing too. EPA recommends retesting at least every two years after mitigation, and after major renovations. A short-term kit is inexpensive, and a continuous digital monitor, like those in our <a href="/best-radon-detector/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best radon detector</a> roundup, lets you see levels at any time and catch a fan failure quickly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Key Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4 pCi/L</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">EPA action level to fix your home</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum wait before post-mitigation test</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2 years</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Recommended retest interval after mitigation</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="contractor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Do You Need a Certified Contractor, or Can You Do It Yourself?
          </h2>

          <p>
            EPA recommends using a qualified radon mitigation contractor. In the US, the two main national certification programs are the National Radon Proficiency Program (NRPP) and the National Radon Safety Board (NRSB). Many states also run their own radon programs, and some require mitigators to be licensed or certified to work in the state. Your state radon program can tell you what applies and often lists qualified contractors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certified installers work to recognized industry standards for system design, fan placement, exhaust location, labeling and post-installation testing. Those standards matter because a poorly placed exhaust can send radon back toward windows, and a fan installed inside the living space can leak radon indoors. Certification also matters in real estate deals, where buyers and lenders often expect documentation of a professionally installed system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whether you can legally install a system yourself depends on your state and local codes. Some states regulate only work done for others, so a homeowner may be allowed to work on their own home, while building permits or electrical rules may still apply. Check with your state radon program and local building department before buying parts. If you sell the house later, a homeowner-installed system may get more scrutiny.
          </p>

          <p>
            Practically, a do-it-yourself system can save several hundred to a thousand dollars or more, but you take on diagnostics, fan selection, exhaust placement and sealing that professionals do daily. Most homeowners are better served by a certified pro with a reduction guarantee. Get two or three quotes, confirm certification, and ask how they will verify the result.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="retest" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Do You Need Mitigation, and How Do You Confirm It Worked?
          </h2>

          <p>
            EPA recommends fixing your home if the radon level is 4 picocuries per liter or higher, and considering a fix between 2 and 4, since there is no known safe level of radon and lower is better. Radon is the leading cause of lung cancer among nonsmokers according to EPA, and the risk rises with level and length of exposure. If you have health concerns about past exposure, talk to your doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            After the system is installed, test again. EPA guidance is to run a short-term test no sooner than 24 hours after the system starts operating and within 30 days. Many contractors include this test or set it up with an independent tester. Use closed-house conditions, just as with the original test, so the result reflects real performance.
          </p>

          <p>
            A well-designed system commonly brings levels well below 4 pCi/L, often below 2. If the post-mitigation result is still high, the contractor should add suction, adjust the fan or seal additional entry points, and a good warranty covers that work. Ask about this before you sign, because a reduction guarantee is one of the most valuable things a quote can include.
          </p>

          <p>
            After that, retest at least every two years, after major renovations or foundation work, and whenever you start using a lower level as living space. Keep the installer's documentation, test results and fan warranty together. If you sell, those records show buyers that radon was measured, fixed and verified. A continuous monitor makes ongoing checks easy and alerts you if something changes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on sealing alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealing cracks and joints is part of a good system, but EPA notes that sealing by itself has not been shown to lower radon significantly or consistently. Radon finds new paths through openings you cannot reach. Use sealing to support active soil depressurization, not to replace it, or you may pay for a fix that does not work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the post-mitigation test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A system that looks right is not proof that levels are down. EPA recommends testing no sooner than 24 hours after the system starts and within 30 days. Use closed-house conditions and, ideally, an independent tester. If the result is still high, the installer should adjust the system under the warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hiring an uncertified installer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Exhaust placement, fan location and sealing all affect whether a system works and whether it is safe. Check that the contractor holds NRPP or NRSB certification or your state's required license. Ask for references and a written reduction guarantee. A cheap installation that misses the target costs more once it has to be redone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a silent fan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Radon fans run constantly and eventually wear out, and a stopped fan gives no obvious sign beyond the manometer reading. Check the gauge every few months. If the liquid levels are even, suction has stopped. A continuous radon monitor also catches failures quickly, so a dead fan does not go unnoticed for a year or more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing only once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Radon levels shift with seasons, weather and changes to the house. A single good result years ago does not guarantee today's level. Retest at least every two years after mitigation, after renovations or foundation repairs, and when you finish a basement or start sleeping on a lower level.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who pays for radon mitigation when selling a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is negotiable. In many transactions, a high radon test leads the buyer to request that the seller install a system before closing or offer a credit. Some state rules or local customs influence the outcome. Buyers often want a certified installer and a passing post-mitigation test, so ask your agent how deals in your area usually handle it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a radon mitigation system increase home value?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It does not usually add value the way a renovation would, but it can remove an obstacle. In areas where radon testing is common in sales, a documented system with a passing test can make a home easier to sell and avoid last-minute negotiations. Keep the installer's paperwork and recent test results.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can radon mitigation be installed in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Installers work year-round, and winter is often when radon levels are highest, which makes testing and verification meaningful. Exterior pipe runs can be installed in cold weather, although frozen ground or snow may complicate exterior work. Ask how the contractor prevents condensation from freezing in exterior pipes and fans during cold snaps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How noisy is a radon fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most radon fans are fairly quiet, producing a low hum and some airflow sound in the pipe. Noise depends on fan size, location and how the pipe is mounted. Fans in an attic or garage are rarely noticed indoors. If noise matters, ask the installer about fan placement, vibration isolation and pipe routing away from bedrooms.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is radon in water a separate cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Radon in well water is treated with different equipment, such as aeration systems or activated carbon filters, which are priced separately from an air mitigation system. Test your water if you have a private well in a high-radon area and your state recommends it. Your state radon program can advise on water testing and treatment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does radon mitigation installation take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical sub-slab system in a straightforward home is often installed in a day, sometimes in a few hours. Crawl space membranes, multiple suction points, interior pipe routes and finished basements can stretch the job to two days. The post-mitigation test then runs for at least a couple of days after the system has been running for 24 hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does radon mitigation help with basement moisture?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes, as a side effect. Sub-slab suction and a sealed crawl space membrane can reduce the moisture that rises through the slab or soil, and some homeowners notice a drier basement. It is not designed as a waterproofing system, though, so water leaks and drainage problems still need their own fixes.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

